you know him as the pumpkin king and you  definitely don't know him as Sandy Claws  but what do only adults notice about  Jack Skellington  Jack Skellington is known as the Pumpkin  King but what does that title actually  mean Jack certainly isn't a monarch and  Halloween Town already has a leader it's  two-faced mayor sure Jack helps organize  the spooking and scaring that takes  place on Halloween and gives some great  speeches along the way but it's tough to  say what exactly he does for an actual  job so he's a leader who holds no real  political power he supposedly has a  knack for motivating people and he's a  consultant to the powers that be yet  it's hard to put your finger on what  exactly he does for a real job it seems  like Jack Skellington is basically  halloweentown's version of the British  royal family Jack truly is royalty as  his title implies and his status is  complete with the abject boredom that  you'd expect from someone who exists to  be a figurehead no wonder he's jealous  of the hard-working much credited Santa  Claus at least Santa actually gets to do  something  while Tim Burton wrote his delightfully  creepy poem The Nightmare Before  Christmas all the way back in 1982 the  first Hollywood feature film to truly  nail the director's trademark spooky  aesthetic wouldn't come until 1988 with  Beetlejuice it's showtime  many of the details in Beetlejuice are  similar to elements of The Nightmare  Before Christmas when Gina Davis and  Alec Baldwin are brought back in the  Seance scene for example their faces  have the same distinct wrinkly lines  seen on the faces of the Dead in  Halloweentown Beetlejuice uses games as  a way to torture the people similar to  Oogie Boogie's antagonism toward Jack  Skellington and Santa Claus and the  statues coming to life in Beetlejuice  are echoed by the kids walking bathtub  in Nightmare there's even a ghostly hat  decoration in Beetlejuice that looks  eerily similar to Jack Skellington when  Beetlejuice finally gets to have his fun  and haunt the humans he appears wearing  a distinctive hat at the top is a man  with a hollow-eyed skull in a familiar  shape adorned by a bat that looks a lot  like Jack's bow tie whether this hat  topper is based on idea for Jack  Skellington or the other way around the  resemblance is unmistakable  Jack's black and white suit looks like  it's glued on and getting it on and off  must be a straight up nightmare moreover  when Jack's Santa suit is destroyed when  his sled is blown up by the military  later in the movie It's revealed that he  was wearing his pinstripe suit under the  Santa suit the whole time therefore it  would seem Jack never takes his suit off  and we don't know if he actually can if  he can't that would make sense for more  than just aesthetic reasons getting back  to the fact that Jack is a skeleton and  skeletons are held together by soft  tissue that eventually rots away you  could argue that he would fall apart  without a suit obviously there's already  a lot of suspension of disbelief that  needs to occur in order to accept most  of what we see in Halloweentown yet it  would make sense that Jack's suit also  serves the purpose of keeping his bones  together just like skin keeps our  insides from falling out in a sense Jack  Skellington has black and white  pinstriped skin like some sort of  bipedal zebra  why does Jack task lock shock and barrel  with kidnapping the holiday icon he  mistakenly refers to as Sandy Claus even  more alarmingly why does he assume that  the kids also refer to as boogies boys  wouldn't go right to Oogie Boogie and  Spill the Beans seriously for a  character who's so smart that the mayor  needs his guidance on the yearly  Halloween plans you've got to wonder why  would Jack ever trust these impish  trick-or-treaters for one thing they're  not the sharpest tools in the shed since  they accidentally kidnapped the Easter  Bunny instead of Santa secondly  everybody in Halloweentown loves Jack  and holds him in the highest esteem  surely he could have found an eager and  loyal helper anywhere else so why would  he instead pick three kids who at best  treat him with amused disdain it's not  like Jack doesn't know about their  association with Oogie Boogie and one  more thing  that no account Oogie Boogie out of this  so he knows where their true loyalties  lie but decides to count on them anyway  it seems that jack isn't as clever as  the residence of Halloweentown think  as he sings The Number Jack's Lament  Jack removes his head holding it out  like Hamlet holds the exhumed Jester  York skull while reciting his famous  alasporic speech the fact that Jack is a  skeleton and can therefore bend the  rules of his Anatomy allows him to  embody both Yorick and hamlet in the  recreation of this Shakespearean scene  while this self-decapitation lasts just  for a moment during Jack's sweeping song  about identity and futility it speaks  very strongly to who Jack really is one  part of him is Hamlet full of on WE and  self-doubt wondering at the purpose of  his existence but another part of him is  the jester Yorick the silly fool who can  make you laugh and cry who can tumble  and fall and get back up with a big  smile in this one moment Jack shows just  how complicated he truly is  in animation nearly every detail on  screen is drawn on purpose and this goes  especially for a movie produced by a man  known for crafting a very specific  detailed aesthetic style in The  Nightmare Before Christmas Jack  Skellington's hands show the joints  between his knuckles which makes sense  as this emphatic leader is big on hand  gestures and physical articulation but  if he needs joints to move his fingers  how does he move his neck that looks  like one single fused bone rather than a  stack of vertebrae sure it has wrinkles  but there don't appear to be any joints  based simply on how skeletons work  Jack's neck couldn't bend Twist and Turn  without vertebrae after all bones don't  move and bend themselves but instead  achieve Mobility through the combined  efforts of their muscles and Joints how  does the ever introspective Jack Bend  his neck in contemplation and wonderment  then such deep questions hard-hitting  and poignant may never be answered  Disney had some serious apprehensions  about producing The Nightmare Before  Christmas as a children's movie their  concerns about the horror elements LED  Disney to release the film under the  company's grown-up subsidiary Touchstone  pictures instead of the main Disney  label  how horrible our Christmas will be no  how Jolly the entertainment giant also  requested a few changes for example  Disney execs demanded that Jack  Skellington Should Have Eyes in his  sockets they say the eyes are the  windows to the soul a fountain of  emotion that allows people to relate to  each other so perhaps Disney was worried  that Jack would seem lacking in  personality without them or maybe the  studio was worried that Jack Skellington  would look too sinister for younger  audiences luckily producer Creator Tim  Burton and director Henry Selleck stood  their ground on the point that Jack is  in fact a skeleton and skeletons don't  have eyes it's a good thing they stood  strong too because Jack now a beloved  character doesn't seem to have suffered  any kind of hit for having empty eye  sockets in fact his face is so  expressive that kids may not even notice  that he has no eyes even if Disney  definitely did  of course people emote with more than  just their eyes for example a fair bit  of emotion comes across in the eyebrows  and the mouth without eyes Jack has to  depend even more on these kinds of  facial reactions the only problem with  this of course is that skulls can't  actually move muscles and skin create  the appearance of brows going up or down  as well as contorting the lips into a  smile or a Grimace the human skull  doesn't move in real life since bone is  solid certainly there has to be some  artistic license at play to give a  skeleton Without Eyes some semblance of  emotion and to that end the animators  afford Jack's skeleton face the same  flexibility he would have if it were  covered in skin it's almost as if Jack  Skellington's skull is made of clay  there are some weird things going on  with Jack Skellington anatomically  speaking one aspect of his body that  draws a critical eye is that his mouth  appears to be stitched together except  when it's not the lines running up and  down Jack's face could be seen simply as  stylized wrinkly lips at first like the  remnants of Flesh in a state of decay  but if you look toward the back of  Jack's skull his mouth looks like it's  just sewn together when you think about  it this makes sense there are no muscles  to hold to the bottom and the top of his  jaw in place when closed his mouth looks  like it's strung together when open it's  as if the threads have magically split  except towards the back where they hold  the hinges of the jaw together so you  can probably add another unique feature  to Jack's Anatomy apparently string can  be sewn through his bone to take the  place of muscles and tendons Jack  Skellington is truly a medical Marvel  Jack Skellington is a collection of  bones without connective or protective  tissue held together in large part by a  suit possibly with stitches securing his  Jaws you wouldn't think that makes for a  particularly durable body yet Jack gets  hammered by cannon fire and escapes with  little more than a burned Santa suit at  another point he leaps around Oogie's  projectiles and traps like a bona fide  gymnast and even if Jack had been hit he  probably would have come out feeling  fine take the movie's final act for  example the Pumpkin King is blasted out  of the sky by the military and springs  back up almost immediately even  retaining enough strength and power to  return home and save Santa Claus and  Sally perhaps there's some truth to the  idea that you can't die twice  foreign
 

